WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.380
Welcome to Module 11 of the
Non-Person Identity learning path:

2
00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:06.587
deploy to test, then production.

3
00:00:06.587 --> 00:00:09.731
This is the last module,
and it serves every gate:

4
00:00:09.731 --> 00:00:13.438
each step of the runbook names
the gate it moves forward.

5
00:00:13.438 --> 00:00:17.850
It goes with Chapter 11 of the developer
workbook, the deployment runbook.

6
00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:19.050
One thing first.

7
00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:21.705
Chapter 11 is held for the owner's review,

8
00:00:21.705 --> 00:00:26.775
and nobody has run its runbook against
a live tenant, subscription or forest.

9
00:00:26.775 --> 00:00:31.403
Its deploy scripts are tested against
fictional tenants served by a fake,

10
00:00:31.403 --> 00:00:33.375
and their dry-run output is real.

11
00:00:33.375 --> 00:00:36.700
Every live result in the
chapter is labeled Expected.

12
00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:40.785
So this module's Lab 1 runs only
the dry run, on the kit's fixtures,

13
00:00:40.785 --> 00:00:44.510
and you'll read the live
stages rather than run them.

14
00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:49.310
Why a runbook?

15
00:00:49.310 --> 00:00:53.252
Each chapter's Lab 2 proves
one piece in a test tenant,

16
00:00:53.252 --> 00:00:55.360
by hand, and then backs it out.

17
00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:59.535
A deployment has to prove them together,
in an order that can be undone,

18
00:00:59.535 --> 00:01:02.172
against an environment someone depends on.

19
00:01:02.172 --> 00:01:05.811
So the runbook is one set
of commands, run twice.

20
00:01:05.811 --> 00:01:08.025
First against an Entra test tenant,

21
00:01:08.025 --> 00:01:12.073
an Azure test subscription and
an Active Directory test forest.

22
00:01:12.073 --> 00:01:14.785
Then, unchanged, against production.

23
00:01:14.785 --> 00:01:16.735
Only the settings file changes.

24
00:01:16.735 --> 00:01:21.219
In this module you dry-run the scripts
that guard that order: the settings check,

25
00:01:21.219 --> 00:01:23.634
the preflight, the smoke
test, the rollback,

26
00:01:23.634 --> 00:01:27.850
and the evidence check that the
promotion to production depends on.

27
00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:34.438
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

28
00:01:34.438 --> 00:01:36.825
which takes about sixty
minutes with the lab.

29
00:01:36.825 --> 00:01:40.393
You'll explain what the runbook
adds to each chapter's Lab 2,

30
00:01:40.393 --> 00:01:43.663
and why the policies that can
block a sign-in come last.

31
00:01:43.663 --> 00:01:47.083
You'll describe the settings
file's rules: no secret,

32
00:01:47.083 --> 00:01:49.851
no placeholder, and the
stage it says it is.

33
00:01:49.851 --> 00:01:52.658
You'll run the preflight,
the smoke test and the

34
00:01:52.658 --> 00:01:55.613
rollback plan on fixtures,
and read their checks.

35
00:01:55.613 --> 00:01:59.750
You'll show that a rollback plan whose
hash nobody approved sends nothing.

36
00:01:59.750 --> 00:02:03.630
And you'll explain why a fixture stage
can never pass the evidence check,

37
00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:06.787
and what production's preflight
needs from the test stage.

38
00:02:06.787 --> 00:02:11.228
Before you start, finish Modules
1 to 10, or at least their Lab 1s:

39
00:02:11.228 --> 00:02:14.799
this module doesn't teach
the kit's chapters again.

40
00:02:14.799 --> 00:02:20.620
Module 10 matters most, for the evidence
package and the first-week drift runs.

41
00:02:24.370 --> 00:02:26.558
Unit 1 starts with the safe order.

42
00:02:26.558 --> 00:02:30.294
Inventory and owners come
first, and they only read.

43
00:02:30.294 --> 00:02:35.383
Then the app management policy, scoped to
a pilot application and a synthetic one.

44
00:02:35.383 --> 00:02:39.674
Microsoft Learn documents no
report-only mode for these policies,

45
00:02:39.674 --> 00:02:43.889
but it says a policy refuses only
a create or update that breaks it,

46
00:02:43.889 --> 00:02:47.557
so an existing secret keeps
working while the pilot runs.

47
00:02:47.557 --> 00:02:50.769
Then a federated credential
and a managed identity

48
00:02:50.769 --> 00:02:53.458
on the pilot workload,
beside the old secret.

49
00:02:53.458 --> 00:02:56.676
Then a group managed service
account on a test host,

50
00:02:56.676 --> 00:02:59.895
once the key distribution
root key has replicated.

51
00:02:59.895 --> 00:03:05.003
And last, Conditional Access for workload
identities, in report-only mode, which

52
00:03:05.003 --> 00:03:10.420
Learn's Conditional Access documentation
lets you use to estimate a policy's effect.

53
00:03:10.420 --> 00:03:15.183
In production it's enabled wave by
wave, after a reviewed sign-in summary.

54
00:03:15.183 --> 00:03:18.205
The runbook decides
nothing the gates decide;

55
00:03:18.205 --> 00:03:21.670
it deploys the controls
that let each gate pass.

56
00:03:25.420 --> 00:03:27.958
Before you start, three
things are in place.

57
00:03:27.958 --> 00:03:32.146
Roles: every write in the runbook is
a named person's delegated sign-in,

58
00:03:32.146 --> 00:03:37.364
with a role activated through Privileged
Identity Management for the change window,

59
00:03:37.364 --> 00:03:41.332
which Microsoft Learn's P I M
documentation shows how to activate.

60
00:03:41.332 --> 00:03:45.645
The scheduled and read steps sign
in as an app with a certificate.

61
00:03:45.645 --> 00:03:47.595
Nothing signs in with a secret.

62
00:03:47.595 --> 00:03:51.970
That rule is this workbook's design
guidance, from the kit's write paths.

63
00:03:51.970 --> 00:03:55.749
Approvals: the change advisory
board approves the change record,

64
00:03:55.749 --> 00:03:59.318
the identity governance officer
approves the settings file,

65
00:03:59.318 --> 00:04:02.677
a reviewer who isn't the operator
approves each plan's hash,

66
00:04:02.677 --> 00:04:07.295
and the ISSO and the change advisory
board approve the promotion to production.

67
00:04:07.295 --> 00:04:09.308
And one change record per stage.

68
00:04:09.308 --> 00:04:11.528
Its number goes into the settings file,

69
00:04:11.528 --> 00:04:15.090
and every manifest the deploy
scripts write repeats it.

70
00:04:18.840 --> 00:04:22.115
The settings file is
one JSON file per stage.

71
00:04:22.115 --> 00:04:27.584
It holds every value the commands need:
the tenant, the cloud, the identities,

72
00:04:27.584 --> 00:04:32.965
the operators, the pilot, the synthetic
objects, the policies and the waves.

73
00:04:32.965 --> 00:04:35.493
The kit checks it before any command runs,

74
00:04:35.493 --> 00:04:38.690
against four rules, which
are this workbook's design.

75
00:04:38.690 --> 00:04:41.981
No secrets: a field whose
name looks like a secret,

76
00:04:41.981 --> 00:04:46.752
or a value that looks like a client
secret or a private key, stops the run.

77
00:04:46.752 --> 00:04:50.631
The identities are named by client
I D and certificate thumbprint,

78
00:04:50.631 --> 00:04:54.227
and the private key stays in the
operator's certificate store.

79
00:04:54.227 --> 00:04:57.122
No placeholders: a value still shaped like

80
00:04:57.122 --> 00:05:00.015
an angle-bracketed tenant
I D stops the run.

81
00:05:00.015 --> 00:05:02.614
The stage it says it is:
the file's environment

82
00:05:02.614 --> 00:05:05.353
must match the stage you
tell the command to run.

83
00:05:05.353 --> 00:05:07.641
And the same commands for both stages:

84
00:05:07.641 --> 00:05:11.990
moving from test to production
changes the file, and nothing else.

85
00:05:15.740 --> 00:05:19.665
The runbook adds five scripts, each
with a fixture mode that serves

86
00:05:19.665 --> 00:05:23.590
Graph and Active Directory from a
file and sends nothing anywhere.

87
00:05:23.590 --> 00:05:26.428
The preflight runs checks
P F oh one to eleven,

88
00:05:26.428 --> 00:05:31.415
writes a snapshot that the rollback
restores, and writes nothing to the tenant.

89
00:05:31.415 --> 00:05:34.449
The smoke test runs checks
S T oh one to oh seven

90
00:05:34.449 --> 00:05:38.440
against a synthetic service
principal and service account.

91
00:05:38.440 --> 00:05:41.941
Its one write is a probe
that's expected to be refused:

92
00:05:41.941 --> 00:05:44.140
a new secret on the synthetic app.

93
00:05:44.140 --> 00:05:45.952
The rollback has two modes.

94
00:05:45.952 --> 00:05:48.564
Plan writes the plan and prints its hash.

95
00:05:48.564 --> 00:05:52.711
Apply checks the approved hash,
the tenant and a seven-day age,

96
00:05:52.711 --> 00:05:56.028
then dry-runs, or sends
only with the apply switch.

97
00:05:56.028 --> 00:06:01.152
It patches policies and enables or
disables accounts, and it never deletes.

98
00:06:01.152 --> 00:06:05.302
The evidence writer records a step
that ran an earlier chapter's script.

99
00:06:05.302 --> 00:06:08.330
And the evidence check is the go or no-go.

100
00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:13.993
Unit 2 is the stages.

101
00:06:13.993 --> 00:06:17.794
The live test stage
prepares synthetic objects,

102
00:06:17.794 --> 00:06:22.642
runs the preflight, and deploys in
the safe order, Steps 1.1 to 1.5.

103
00:06:22.642 --> 00:06:25.873
Then it runs the smoke
test, captures the evidence,

104
00:06:25.873 --> 00:06:28.630
rehearses the rollback, and deploys again.

105
00:06:28.630 --> 00:06:32.794
Production starts only when every
go or no-go criterion is signed,

106
00:06:32.794 --> 00:06:37.806
nine rows of them, and its preflight adds
one check that runs only in production:

107
00:06:37.806 --> 00:06:40.582
P F eleven, which needs
the test stage's smoke

108
00:06:40.582 --> 00:06:43.743
test to have passed every
check, in another tenant.

109
00:06:43.743 --> 00:06:46.443
Then production enables Conditional Access

110
00:06:46.443 --> 00:06:48.930
for workload identities
one wave at a time.

111
00:06:48.930 --> 00:06:53.316
Remember the status: nobody has run
these stages against a live tenant,

112
00:06:53.316 --> 00:06:56.417
and every live result in
them is labeled Expected.

113
00:06:56.417 --> 00:06:59.730
That's why Lab 1 runs only the dry run.

114
00:07:03.480 --> 00:07:04.205
Try it.

115
00:07:04.205 --> 00:07:08.942
Lab 1 runs in the non-person
identity folder, in PowerShell 7.

116
00:07:08.942 --> 00:07:13.052
The test and production tenants
are fictional and served by a fake,

117
00:07:13.052 --> 00:07:15.443
so nothing signs in and nothing is sent.

118
00:07:15.443 --> 00:07:19.035
Step one loads the deploy
module and checks the template,

119
00:07:19.035 --> 00:07:20.717
and it's refused on purpose.

120
00:07:20.717 --> 00:07:23.084
You get an error, then
every problem at once,

121
00:07:23.084 --> 00:07:27.005
starting with the environment
placeholder that hasn't been replaced.

122
00:07:27.005 --> 00:07:29.567
A filled-in file prints one line instead.

123
00:07:29.567 --> 00:07:32.379
Step two runs the test stage's preflight.

124
00:07:32.379 --> 00:07:35.068
It prints twelve passes and one warning.

125
00:07:35.068 --> 00:07:40.361
The warning is P F oh six: the fixture's
Conditional Access administrator holds a

126
00:07:40.361 --> 00:07:44.132
standing assignment, not one
that's eligible through P I M,

127
00:07:44.132 --> 00:07:47.180
and the preflight reports
it but doesn't block.

128
00:07:47.180 --> 00:07:52.330
It writes two evidence records, and
its last line says go for this stage.

129
00:07:56.080 --> 00:07:58.603
Step three runs the smoke test against the

130
00:07:58.603 --> 00:08:01.630
synthetic service principal
and service account.

131
00:08:01.630 --> 00:08:05.621
It prints eight passes, for
checks S T oh one to oh seven,

132
00:08:05.621 --> 00:08:09.068
with oh five run once
for each workload policy.

133
00:08:09.068 --> 00:08:13.167
The synthetic app is in the register
with two owners and no secret.

134
00:08:13.167 --> 00:08:16.330
The synthetic service account
is found and rated high.

135
00:08:16.330 --> 00:08:20.718
The app management policy refuses
a new secret on the synthetic app.

136
00:08:20.718 --> 00:08:24.168
The federated credential
matches the settings exactly.

137
00:08:24.168 --> 00:08:28.255
Each workload policy assigns the
synthetic service principal directly.

138
00:08:28.255 --> 00:08:31.280
The synthetic sign-ins
show the policies' result.

139
00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:34.918
And the test host can retrieve the
group managed account's password.

140
00:08:34.918 --> 00:08:37.543
Read S T oh three closely.

141
00:08:37.543 --> 00:08:42.269
It says the fixture's refusal is real
code unverified, because Microsoft

142
00:08:42.269 --> 00:08:47.040
Learn doesn't give the exact error
an app management policy returns.

143
00:08:50.790 --> 00:08:52.852
Steps four to six are the rollback.

144
00:08:52.852 --> 00:08:55.645
Step four plans it from
the preflight's snapshot,

145
00:08:55.645 --> 00:08:57.740
with the synthetic objects' cleanup.

146
00:08:57.740 --> 00:09:00.968
Seven actions: turn the
two workload policies off,

147
00:09:00.968 --> 00:09:06.006
restore the tenant's default app management
policy, turn the pilot policy off,

148
00:09:06.006 --> 00:09:10.415
re-enable the legacy account, and
disable the two synthetic objects.

149
00:09:10.415 --> 00:09:12.040
No action is a delete.

150
00:09:12.040 --> 00:09:14.453
The plan prints its hash for the reviewer.

151
00:09:14.453 --> 00:09:18.861
Step five applies the plan with a
hash nobody approved, all zeros,

152
00:09:18.861 --> 00:09:23.602
and it stops: the plan's hash isn't
the approved one, and nothing was sent.

153
00:09:23.602 --> 00:09:28.168
Step six applies it with the plan's
own hash, but without the apply switch,

154
00:09:28.168 --> 00:09:30.800
so it's a dry run: seven
would-apply actions,

155
00:09:30.800 --> 00:09:34.902
with both workload policies going
from report-only to disabled.

156
00:09:34.902 --> 00:09:40.070
In a real stage, a reviewer who isn't
the operator passes you that hash.

157
00:09:43.820 --> 00:09:46.336
Step seven runs production's preflight,

158
00:09:46.336 --> 00:09:49.995
with the test stage's smoke
test as its promotion evidence.

159
00:09:49.995 --> 00:09:54.624
Fourteen passes, including P F
eleven, which runs in production only:

160
00:09:54.624 --> 00:09:59.170
the test stage's smoke test passed,
eight of eight, in another tenant.

161
00:09:59.170 --> 00:10:02.920
Step eight checks the test stage's
evidence, and it says no-go.

162
00:10:02.920 --> 00:10:06.020
Four of nine requirement rows
are present and passing.

163
00:10:06.020 --> 00:10:08.811
Steps 1.1, 1.2, 1.3 and 1.5 are missing,

164
00:10:08.811 --> 00:10:15.145
because they record their evidence
only when they run against a tenant.

165
00:10:15.145 --> 00:10:20.070
And the rollback row fails on dry run,
which the go or no-go doesn't accept.

166
00:10:20.070 --> 00:10:23.608
The exit code is one, and
that's right for a dry run.

167
00:10:23.608 --> 00:10:28.870
Step nine lists the four evidence
records and the K S I's each names.

168
00:10:28.870 --> 00:10:32.910
Step ten runs the deploy
scripts' fifty-five tests.

169
00:10:36.660 --> 00:10:40.260
Lab 2 is the live test
stage, and then production.

170
00:10:40.260 --> 00:10:41.748
Nobody has run it yet.

171
00:10:41.748 --> 00:10:46.896
You need an Entra test tenant with
Workload ID Premium and Entra ID P1 or P2,

172
00:10:46.896 --> 00:10:52.635
an Azure test subscription and an Active
Directory test forest, never production.

173
00:10:52.635 --> 00:10:58.697
You also need an approved change record,
the reader app and the deployment identity,

174
00:10:58.697 --> 00:11:03.969
each with a certificate, operators
eligible for their roles through P I M,

175
00:11:03.969 --> 00:11:07.835
and the second labs of Chapters
1, 4, 5, 6 and 8 already run.

176
00:11:07.835 --> 00:11:12.579
Fill in and check the settings
file, create the synthetic objects

177
00:11:12.579 --> 00:11:17.147
and the pilot policy, run the
preflight, and deploy Steps 1.1 to 1.5.

178
00:11:17.147 --> 00:11:20.058
Then run the smoke test,
capture the evidence,

179
00:11:20.058 --> 00:11:23.911
rehearse the rollback, deploy
again, and check the evidence.

180
00:11:23.911 --> 00:11:26.673
To back it out, Step
1.8 applies the approved

181
00:11:26.673 --> 00:11:30.147
rollback and checks the
tenant against the baseline.

182
00:11:30.147 --> 00:11:31.748
The kit never deletes.

183
00:11:31.748 --> 00:11:36.410
Production comes only when every
go or no-go criterion is signed.

184
00:11:40.160 --> 00:11:41.547
The federal requirement.

185
00:11:41.547 --> 00:11:44.540
Every runbook step ends
with a requirements table,

186
00:11:44.540 --> 00:11:47.460
and the kit writes an
evidence record for each row.

187
00:11:47.460 --> 00:11:52.018
The change record and the settings
file's hash cover NIST special

188
00:11:52.018 --> 00:11:56.660
publication eight hundred fifty-three
C M three, C M five and A C five.

189
00:11:56.660 --> 00:12:01.027
The preflight's checks on the
deployment identity and the reader app

190
00:12:01.027 --> 00:12:05.547
cover authenticator management and
least privilege, I A five and A C six.

191
00:12:05.547 --> 00:12:10.598
Its baseline and policy-state checks cover
continuous monitoring and change testing.

192
00:12:10.598 --> 00:12:16.548
The smoke test covers account, inventory,
identifier, access and audit controls.

193
00:12:16.548 --> 00:12:20.698
The SHA256SUMS file covers A U nine.

194
00:12:20.698 --> 00:12:25.826
And the rollback record covers C M
three and C P ten, system recovery,

195
00:12:25.826 --> 00:12:30.209
but only from an applied rollback;
a dry run isn't accepted.

196
00:12:30.209 --> 00:12:34.121
K S I's are FedRAMP twenty X
indicators for cloud providers,

197
00:12:34.121 --> 00:12:37.135
and every alignment is
the program's reading.

198
00:12:37.135 --> 00:12:41.470
The assessor and the authorizing
official decide each control.

199
00:12:45.220 --> 00:12:47.195
Check yourself with two questions.

200
00:12:47.195 --> 00:12:51.482
First: the rollback's plan hash
isn't the one the reviewer approved.

201
00:12:51.482 --> 00:12:52.957
What does apply mode do?

202
00:12:52.957 --> 00:12:54.182
The answer is b.

203
00:12:54.182 --> 00:12:57.832
It stops before it sends anything,
and says nothing was sent.

204
00:12:57.832 --> 00:12:59.382
You saw it in step five.

205
00:12:59.382 --> 00:13:03.708
Second: in the dry run, why does
the evidence check end no-go?

206
00:13:03.708 --> 00:13:04.806
The answer is b.

207
00:13:04.806 --> 00:13:10.207
Steps 1.1 to 1.5 record their evidence
only when they run against a tenant,

208
00:13:10.207 --> 00:13:15.608
and a dry-run rollback records dry run,
which the go or no-go doesn't accept.

209
00:13:15.608 --> 00:13:19.920
So a fixture stage can never pass the
evidence check, and that's by design.

210
00:13:19.920 --> 00:13:22.347
The module page has three more questions,

211
00:13:22.347 --> 00:13:25.210
each linked to the
section that answers it.

212
00:13:28.960 --> 00:13:29.785
To sum up.

213
00:13:29.785 --> 00:13:34.786
You dry-ran the runbook's own scripts: a
settings file refused for its placeholders,

214
00:13:34.786 --> 00:13:39.346
a preflight that says go with one warning,
a smoke test on synthetic objects,

215
00:13:39.346 --> 00:13:43.538
a rollback plan refused without its
approved hash and dry-run with it,

216
00:13:43.538 --> 00:13:47.362
a production preflight that checks
the test stage's smoke test,

217
00:13:47.362 --> 00:13:50.672
and an evidence check that
says no-go on a fixture stage.

218
00:13:50.672 --> 00:13:55.798
The evidence an ISSO keeps for
each stage is the evidence folder,

219
00:13:55.798 --> 00:14:01.988
with one record per requirement row, the
run folders they cover, the operator's

220
00:14:01.988 --> 00:14:08.372
input files, and the SHA256SUMS file, which
the ISSO checks with sha256sum dash c.

221
00:14:08.372 --> 00:14:11.834
Before you run a stage, work
through the chapter's checklist.

222
00:14:11.834 --> 00:14:13.610
And that's the end of the path.

223
00:14:13.610 --> 00:14:18.590
Go back to the learning path, or to the
Labs page for every lab in the workbook.
